{
  "summary": "The prior rounds have thoroughly debated extraction boundaries but have converged without resolving a key tension: the discussion treats Tier 1 as the obvious first move while simultaneously acknowledging it yields only a ~4.5% line reduction. The actual highest-value first commit is the one the sixth contributor identified — fixing the _feature_markdown escaping bug (line 271 only escapes '['/']', missing '{'/'}'/'\\'%) and deduplicating the 6 inline escaping blocks in _build_feature_detail_widgets (lines 336, 345, 354, 371, 380, 389) that replicate what _escape_markup already does. This is a concrete, testable bug fix that also cleans the extraction boundary for the subsequent Tier 1 split. However, the discussion has a blind spot about the actual module extraction mechanics: every prior round assumes tui_helpers.py can be a 'pure, framework-free' module, but _handle_config_command (line 111) instantiates Config() and _handle_context_command (line 156) calls view_context_file/edit_context_file which invoke subprocess/editor — these are not pure functions. They're CLI-path functions that happen to live at module scope. The honest Tier 1 split is not 'pure helpers' vs 'everything else' but 'functions that don't reference PipelineApp or Textual' vs 'methods on PipelineApp'. That's still a valuable separation (it isolates CLI entry points and formatting utilities from the UI class) but it should be named accurately — tui_cli.py or tui_utils.py, not tui_helpers.py — because the testing benefit is 'testable without Textual' not 'testable without any imports.' For _check_for_changes, the sixth contributor's suggestion to pass the already-loaded features list to the server push instead of calling list_all() again is the correct minimal fix — line 2636 can simply reuse the features variable from line 2606 filtered to the current board, plus a single FeatureFile.list_all() call for the server push that shares the same parsed cache. But more fundamentally, the double-call pattern reveals that _check_for_changes is doing two conceptually separate things (local UI refresh and server state sync) that should be decoupled: the server push should be on its own timer or triggered by the successful completion of a local refresh, not interleaved within the same method. This is a small structural improvement that naturally falls out of any Tier 2 extraction work and should be noted as a dependency."
}
